# Santy Anna: traditional capstan shanty. Words and tune from Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (1921), No. 8
# (F minor there; here in A minor). The minor tune is Terry's own 1921 print, not a revival setting.
{title: Santy Anna}
{composer: Traditional (sea shanty)}
{key: Am}
{tempo: 116}
{time: 4/4}
{x_level: 2}
{x_strum: D - D U - U D U}

{comment: Solo lines and chorus lines alternate: everyone sings "Way-ah, me Santy Anna" and "All on the plains of Mexico".}

{start_of_grid label="Verse"}
| Am . . . | C . G . |
| Am . . . | F . G . |
| C . G . | Am . Em . |
| Am . Em . | Am . . . |
{end_of_grid}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
Oh [Am]Santy Anna [C]won the [G]day.
Way-[Am]Ah, me Santy [F]An[G]na.
Oh [C]Santy [G]Anna [Am]won the [Em]day.
All on [Am]the plains [Em]of [Am]Mexico.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
He [Am]beat the Prooshans [C]fair[G]ly.
Way-[Am]Ah, me Santy [F]An[G]na.
And [C]whacked the [G]British [Am]near[Em]ly.
All on [Am]the plains [Em]of [Am]Mexico.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
He [Am]was a rorty [C]gine[G]ral;
Way-[Am]Ah, me Santy [F]An[G]na.
A [C]rorty [G]snorty [Am]gine[Em]ral.
All on [Am]the plains [Em]of [Am]Mexico.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 4"}
They [Am]took him out and [C]shot [G]him.
Way-[Am]Ah, me Santy [F]An[G]na.
Oh [C]when shall [G]we for[Am]get [Em]him.
All on [Am]the plains [Em]of [Am]Mexico.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 5"}
Oh [Am]Santy Anna [C]won the [G]day
Way-[Am]Ah, me Santy [F]An[G]na.
And [C]Gin'ral [G]Taylor [Am]run a[Em]way.
All on [Am]the plains [Em]of [Am]Mexico.
{end_of_verse}
{x_end: Am}
